  "query_preview": "..Tonight into Monday features dry and seasonable\nconditions before a shortwave trough and storm system moves\nonshore. There is moderate confidence for some patchy fog may be \npossible again early Monday morning, though there will be less\nboundary layer moisture to work with than yesterday. The incoming \nsystem will last all day Tuesday and depart the region Wednesday \nmorning. Current QPF amounts look close to the last storm, with\nupslope enhancement along the southern edge of the Mogollon Rim,\nSan Fransisco Peaks and Kaibab Plateau. Modest instability during\nthe day Tuesday makes for a slight chance for embedded \nthunderstorms with showers. With a more northerly origin from this\nstorm, snow levels will be lower, dropping down to 6500-7000ft \nTuesday night. As the snow level drop occurs later in the storm, \naccumulations will be light with only the Kaibab Plateau, San \nFrancisco Peaks and highest parts of the White Mountains seeing \nanything over an inch. There is potential for minor impacts from a\ndusting of snow or icy conditions in the Flagstaff area Tuesday \nnight. High confidence exists for windy conditions Tuesday, \nparticularly just downslope/north of the Mogollon Rim with gusts \nover 35 mph common.\n\nWednesday will see another break with temperatures recovering to\nnear seasonal averages before the next trough moves in Thursday\nafternoon and lasts into late Friday night. This is favored to be \na slightly colder storm system with snow levels as low as 6000ft.\nThe trend among ensemble models has overall been drier than \nTuesday's system, though the EC suite has boosted chances in the\nNBM to still be around 30% for the first inch of snow for the \nseason in Flagstaff.",
